    {
      "id": "cmtibnhfi006j04ihu2khfei4",
      "term": "When would $100M of revenue synergies go straight into EBITDA?",
      "setTitle": "M&A ",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "Revenue synergies flow straight into EBITDA only when the incremental revenue carries no incremental cost.",
          "kind": "condition"
        },
        {
          "text": "A price lift is the clean example: raising prices on existing customers adds revenue with no incremental cost, so every dollar falls through to EBITDA.",
          "kind": "example"
        },
        {
          "text": "In software, a company that buys another software company can cross-sell to its customer base, riding on R&D, infrastructure, and maintenance that already exist and are paid for, so the extra revenue needs no new spend.",
          "kind": "example"
        },
        {
          "text": "In the software cross-sell case, no new engineering headcount or hosting cost is needed.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Most revenue synergies do not qualify — realizing cross-sell revenue usually means hiring salespeople, carrying COGS, or spending on marketing.",
          "kind": "contrast"
        },
        {
          "text": "Typically only the margin on the synergistic revenue belongs in EBITDA, not the full $100 million.",
          "kind": "quantitative"
        }
      ],
